{
  "dom_id": "step-q89572",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x+3)\\ge 0,\\,x<-1.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x<-1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-1 \\\\ 2x & <-2 \\\\ 2x-1 & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2x-1$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<-1).$ So, we can divide both sides by $2x-1,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-1)(x+3) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{2x-1} & \\le \\frac{0}{2x-1} \\\\ x+3 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\le -3$ and $x<-1.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\le -3.$",
